              <p>
                For military and cultural history buffs, both sectors have profound merits:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                <li><strong>Ben Dinh</strong> illuminates grassroots tactical survival—how local village partisans adapted the red laterite soil into an impenetrable combat zone right on the doorstep of the U.S. 25th Infantry Division base.</li>
                <li><strong>Ben Duoc</strong> showcases strategic regional coordination—how high-command officers lived and coordinated a multi-province resistance theater from deep underground command bunkers.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                If your objective is to study the technical architecture of traps and tunnels in under two hours, Ben Dinh accomplishes that efficiently.
              </p>
              <p>
                If your objective is to absorb the broader landscape—including the memorial temple, war-zone living reconstructions, and shaded forest trails—Ben Duoc offers a vastly richer canvas.
              </p>
